  "query_preview": "/Through Saturday/...\nIssued at 1222 AM MDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nThere haven't been too many significant changes in the large scale\npattern over the last 24 hours. A broad upper ridge remains\ncentered over the south central CONUS, with a trough noted over\nthe northern Rockies... leaving the area in broad southwesterly\nflow aloft. This also puts us square in the middle of the\nmonsoonal moisture, and there's been a good amount of\nprecipitation falling across northwestern Colorado, which has\nspread as far east as Grand/Jackson county.\n\nOvernight CAMs are actually in decent agreement in how today's\nconvective potential plays out. Most guidance pushes a cluster of\nshowers and thunderstorms across Middle/North Park late this\nmorning into the early afternoon, which then advances east into\nthe Front Range and I-25 corridor by early/mid afternoon. The\nconvective environment is marginally favorable across most of the\nlower elevations, with modest MLCAPE (~500 J/kg) but increased\ndeep layer shear. We can't totally rule out a few strong to severe\nwind gusts with any stronger showers/storms as convection\ncontinues to push eastward through the afternoon and evening\nhours.\n\nMonday still looks to feature the best chance of precipitation\nacross most of our forecast area, as a more pronounced shortwave\nraces across the state during the afternoon/early overnight hours.\nThere's timing differences across the various models, but\nreasonably good agreement that a convective cluster will develop\nacross southwestern Colorado during the day, advancing into our\nforecast area by the mid/late afternoon hours. Like the past few\ndays, meager surface/boundary layer moisture will limit\ninstability, but the stronger synoptic forcing should lead to more\nwidespread rainfall into Monday evening/Monday night. \n\nBy Tuesday, the center of the 500mb ridge should slide east into \nthe Ohio Valley, while a strong closed low spins off the coast of \nBritish Columbia. Across the region, southwesterly flow aloft will\npersist. Moisture will diminish through this timeframe, with \nenough drying by Wednesday and Thursday that only a couple of \nweak showers are in the forecast across the the high country. \n\nThe forecast pattern becomes a bit more complex as we approach\nnext weekend. The closed low near the Pacific Northwest coastline \nwill eventually drift onshore, and its approach towards our area\nshould lead to a more poleward component of the mid/upper level\nwinds. Further complicating the pattern is the potential tropical\ncyclone development across the eastern Pacific, which could\ncontribute to additional tropical moisture advection into the\nregion by next weekend. There is a fairly large spread across the\novernight guidance and it'll likely be a while until we have a\nbetter handle on things, but the overall pattern would suggest\nthat we'll see precipitation chances increase towards the end of\nour forecast period.",
